## Purpose
- Capture DB ownership and migration workflow for auth service.

## Source of truth
- `services/team-hub-auth/team-hub-auth/Data/AuthDbContext.cs`
- `services/team-hub-auth/team-hub-auth/Migrations/*.cs`
- `services/team-hub-auth/team-hub-auth/appsettings*.json`
- `docker-compose.yml` (prod-like stack)
- `docker-compose.dev.yml` (local dev database only)

## Organization database

### Purpose
- Store organization/team/invitation data for `team-hub-organization`.

### Source of truth
- `services/team-hub-organization/team-hub-organization/Data/OrganizationDbContext.cs`
- `services/team-hub-organization/team-hub-organization/Migrations/*`
- `services/team-hub-organization/team-hub-organization/.env.example`
- `docker-compose.yml` / `docker-compose.dev.yml`

### Connection strings
- local dev (`dotnet run`, `docker-compose.dev.yml`): `Host=localhost;Port=5433;Database=organization_db;Username=matsma`
- docker production (`ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production`): `Host=postgres;Port=5432;Database=organizationdb;Username=matsma`

### DB startup
- `docker compose -f docker-compose.dev.yml up -d`, then `dotnet run` — Postgres init script creates `organization_db`; EF `Database.Migrate()` applies schema on startup.
- `docker compose up` — Postgres init script creates `organizationdb`; EF `Database.Migrate()` in organization container applies schema on startup.

## Organization API

See `docs/organization.mb` for `/api/organizations` contract.

## Do
- Use PostgreSQL for auth data only (users).
- Use Redis for refresh-token sessions (`auth:session:*`); Redis is shared infrastructure (`infrastructure/redis/`).
- Treat `AuthDbContext` as schema contract (`users`, indexes).
- Create schema changes through EF Core migrations in `Migrations/`.
- Keep connection string environment-aware:
  - local dev (`dotnet run`, `appsettings.Development.json`): `Host=localhost;Port=5433;Database=auth_db;Username=matsma`
  - docker auth container (`appsettings.Production.json`): `Host=postgres;Port=5432;Database=authdb;Username=matsma`
- Run dev database via `docker compose -f docker-compose.dev.yml up -d` (`postgres-dev`, `team-hub-redis-dev`, volume `postgres_data_dev`); init script creates both `auth_db` and `organization_db`.
- Run prod-like database via `docker compose up` (`postgres-prod`, volume `postgres_data_prod`); init script creates both `authdb` and `organizationdb`.

## Don't
- Do not edit migration history manually unless fixing broken migration state.
- Do not use direct SQL schema changes outside migrations.
- Do not assume postgres on host port `5432` for this project setup.

## Checklist
- New/changed model has migration and snapshot update.
- DB startup path still works with `docker-compose.dev.yml` and `docker-compose.yml` postgres services.
- Unique constraints (username) remain intentional.
